14 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 0
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 28 0 R
/Alt (A study explores early post-transplant urinary EGF as a predictor of kidney transplant recipients' long-term allograft loss. The setting involves a single-center cohort and temporal validation cohort with 290 and 203 participants, respectively. Factors linked to three-month uEGF include eGFR, age, interstitial fibrosis, and sex. The main cohort shows uEGF association with allograft loss, enhancing prediction models with bootstrap validation. The validation cohort shows no eGFR-independent association. A graph displays the log uEGF-to-creatinine ratio versus relative hazard ratio for kidney failure. Study by Creon A., published in Transplant International 2025.)
>>
endobj
15 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 0
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 30 0 R
/Alt (Flowchart of patient selection for analysis. Eligible patients \(n=485\) were transplanted between June 13, 2009, and June 6, 2012. Exclusions: 195 patients \(deceased 7, early allograft loss 12, lost to follow-up or transferred 71, missing urine sample 105\). Included in cross-sectional analysis: 290 patients. Excluded from cross-sectional analysis: 1 patient \(missing date of last follow-up\). Included in time-to-event analysis: 289 patients.)
>>
endobj
16 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 0
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 33 0 R
/Alt (Graphs display cumulative incidence and ROC curves. Panel A shows three uEGF tertiles with significant cumulative incidence differences over 10 years \(Gray's test: p<0.001\). Panel B shows similar tertiles with non-significant differences \(p=0.43\). Panel C features time-dependent ROC curves, with true positive rates against false positive rates, and AUC values ranging from 0.631 to 0.778.)
>>
endobj
17 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 0
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 34 0 R
/Alt (Panel A shows a random forest analysis, with eGFR as the most significant factor, followed by donor and recipient age. Panel B presents a scatter plot illustrating a positive correlation between uEGF/creatinine and eGFR, with a Spearman correlation coefficient of 0.65 and a p-value less than 0.0001.)
>>
endobj
18 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 1
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 34 0 R
/Alt (Forest plot showing hazard ratios \(HR\) and 95% confidence intervals \(CI\) for allograft loss based on various model covariates. Covariates include uEGF alone and combinations with recipient sex, MFI, eGFR, donor age, and recipient age. Hazard ratios range from 0.19 to 0.44, with bars indicating confidence intervals. A dashed vertical line marks HR of 1.0.)
>>
endobj
19 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 0
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 35 0 R
/Alt (Graph showing the relationship between the uEGF-to-creatinine ratio and the relative hazard ratio for allograft loss. The curve decreases from approximately 6 on the y-axis to below 1, with a shaded area indicating confidence intervals. Ticks along the x-axis represent data distribution.)
>>
endobj
20 0 obj
<<
/A 38 0 R
/K 1
/P 6 0 R
/S /Figure
/T (Figure)
/Pg 35 0 R
/Alt (A calibration plot for 7-year allograft loss shows predicted risk on the x-axis and observed risk on the y-axis, both ranging from 0.00 to 1.00. A solid blue line represents the data trend, surrounded by a gray shaded area indicating uncertainty, while a dashed gray line marks the ideal calibration.)
>>
endobj
